        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Simplify <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{x^2-4x+4}{x^2+6x+9} }.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Simplify [MATH: \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{x^2-4x+4}{x^2+6x+9} }.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To simplify the expression, we first factor the numerator and denominator under the radical:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: sqrt((x^{2} \u2212 4x + 4)/(x^{2} + 6x + 9))|= sqrt((x^{2} + 2 \u22c5 x \u22c5 (\u2212 2) + (\u2212 2)^{2})/(x^{2} + 2 \u22c5 x \u22c5 3 + 3^{2})); = sqrt(((x \u2212 2)^{2})/((x + 3)^{2}))]</span></p><p>Then, by applying the quotient rule for radicals and the rules of absolute value, we get</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: sqrt(((x \u2212 2)^{2})/((x + 3)^{2}))|= (sqrt((x \u2212 2)^{2}))/(sqrt((x + 3)^{2})); = (|x \u2212 2|)/(|x + 3|); =|(x \u2212 2)/(x + 3)|.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "To simplify the expression, we first factor the numerator and denominator under the radical:\n\n[MATH: sqrt((x^{2} \u2212 4x + 4)/(x^{2} + 6x + 9))|= sqrt((x^{2} + 2 \u22c5 x \u22c5 (\u2212 2) + (\u2212 2)^{2})/(x^{2} + 2 \u22c5 x \u22c5 3 + 3^{2})); = sqrt(((x \u2212 2)^{2})/((x + 3)^{2}))]\n\nThen, by applying the quotient rule for radicals and the rules of absolute value, we get\n\n[MATH: sqrt(((x \u2212 2)^{2})/((x + 3)^{2}))|= (sqrt((x \u2212 2)^{2}))/(sqrt((x + 3)^{2})); = (|x \u2212 2|)/(|x + 3|); =|(x \u2212 2)/(x + 3)|.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
